From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 05:55:31 +0200
Subject: dma-direct: allocate from ZONE_DMA where only it has an uncached
 alias

arch_dma_set_uncached() is allowed to fail, and dma_direct_alloc()
handles that by freeing the pages and returning NULL. On an architecture
that can alias all of memory uncached this never happens. On one that
can only alias part of it, it happens or not depending on which pages
the buddy allocator had to hand -- the same allocation from the same
driver succeeds on a freshly booted machine and fails once the free
lists have drifted upwards, and what the caller sees is an -ENOMEM on a
machine with most of its memory free.

Nothing rescues it either. The retry ladder in
__dma_direct_alloc_pages() steps down through the zones on
dma_coherent_ok(), which tests the device's addressing limit; a device
whose mask covers all of memory passes that test for exactly the pages
that have no uncached alias, so the ladder never runs.

An architecture in that position already has a way to say where the
alias exists: size ZONE_DMA to it. Then this is just a matter of asking
for the right zone before allocating rather than discovering the answer
afterwards, when the only remaining move is to fail.

Made opt-in, because it is only correct for architectures that have
placed ZONE_DMA deliberately. Selecting
ARCH_DMA_SET_UNCACHED_ZONE_DMA_ONLY is the statement that ZONE_DMA is
the aliasable window; everyone else is unaffected, and where the symbol
is off the IS_ENABLED() compiles the whole thing away.

This is close to what arch code used to do for itself, and on the
platform that prompted it the vendor kernel still does. MIPS massaged
the gfp flags of coherent allocations before the dma-direct conversion
took the decision over, and AVM's 4.9 tree for this SoC keeps that code:
massage_gfp_flags() in arch/mips/mm/dma-default.c adds __GFP_DMA to
every coherent allocation made for a device that is not hardware
coherent, unconditionally, without ever consulting coherent_dma_mask.
What is added here is the same rule reached from the other side. The
CONFIG_ARCH_HAS_DMA_SET_UNCACHED plus !dev_is_dma_coherent(dev) gate
that dma_direct_alloc() already computes is exactly the statement "this
allocation is about to be handed back as an uncached alias", which is
the same set of allocations the vendor's single coherent = 1 call site
covers. One rule, expressed once and generically, rather than per
architecture.

Note what is deliberately not used, because both alternatives look at
first like they would do.

zone_dma_limit cannot express this. dma_direct_optimal_gfp_mask()
returns GFP_DMA only where a device's own phys_limit falls below
zone_dma_limit, and the masters on the affected platform are ordinary
32-bit ones whose phys_limit is 0xffffffff -- above any honest value of
a variable whose meaning is where ZONE_DMA ends. Raising it far enough
to trigger the test would be a false statement about the zone, and
dma_coherent_ok() and the retry ladder read the same variable and would
be wrong with it. The constraint here is not an addressing limit, which
is why the addressing machinery has nowhere to put it.

dev->bus_dma_limit would confine the allocation, and would additionally
constrain streaming mappings -- which have no such problem, because
dma_map_*() never takes an uncached alias of anything. Narrowing the
device's addressing limit to describe a constraint that applies only to
coherent allocations would break the DMA that currently works.

The arch-side guard in arch_dma_set_uncached() stays exactly as loud as
it was. With this in place it should never fire, which makes it an
assertion rather than a condition: if it does, something reached the
allocator by a path this does not cover, and that is worth a message and
a backtrace.

The cost is real and worth stating. These allocations now all come from
one zone instead of from anywhere, so a workload that exhausts or
fragments ZONE_DMA gets an honest allocation failure where previously it
had a chance of succeeding. That is the intended trade: on the affected
platform the alternative outcome was not success but a write into the
register space aliased at the same address.

